Abstract
Pt nanoparticles immobilized on zirconium oxide $(Z\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2})$ and porous carbons (Pt/ZrO2/PCs) tri-component nanohybrids derived from $\mathrm{Pt}/\$ metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) were synthesized. They were prepared by using Pt/MOFs as a template. Additionally, MOFs (UiO-66, a traditional MOFs) were used as $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ and carbon sources without the need of additional precursors. The formation of these composite materials was confirmed through a comprehensive characterization such as transmission electron microscopy, X-ray diffraction, and Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy. The $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ show strong affinity toward the phosphate group and highly electrocatalytic activity for nitro compound on methyl parathion (MP)
molecules. The high performance is owing to the combination of unique electrocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ species, excellent conductivity of PCs, and good adsorption properties of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ crystals for MP. The proposed Pt/ZrO2/PCs tri-component nanocomposite sensor realized the ultrasensitive detection of MP with a wide linear range between $3.8{\times}10^{-9}$ and $1.14\times10^{-2}$ mM and a low limit of detection of $1.45{\times}10^{-9}\ \mathrm{mM}$ . Therefore, it can be developed as an effective sensing platform for the detection of MP.
Keywords: Methyl parathion; Platinum nanoparticles; Zirconia oxide; Porous carbons; Electrochemical sensor
## 1. Introduction
Methyl parathion (MP), as one of the typical organophosphorus pesticides, is universally used in agriculture to promote production and quality [1]. However, MP is a highly neurotoxic substance, which disrupts the activity of acetylcholinesterase in the biomass [2-4]. A large amount of MP has been released into the environment annually due to reckless use. Consequently, there is an urgent need to develop an accurate and sensitive measurement to detect traces of MP, which is tremendous important for human health and environmental protection. A variety of analytical methods for detecting MP have been developed in the last several years, including liquid or gas chromatography coupled with mass spectrometry [5, 6], high-performance liquid chromatography [7-9], and chemiluminescence [10, 11]. However, these methods usually require plenty of time, expensive instruments, complex sample pretreatment and complicated operations thereby limiting their applications. Compared with these technologies, electrochemical methods have many advantages for detecting MP, such as low cost, fast response, good stability and easy operation.
Zirconium oxide $(Z\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2})$ has been used as a capturing agent for selectively enriching free MP owing to its strong affinity to phosphorus groups [12-14]. However, the electrochemical applications of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ were limited because of their poor conductivity. Thus, nanostructured $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ crystals with multiple nanocomponents, especially carbon-based materials are of interest. Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been widely developed as novel precursors for preparing nanostructured porous carbons (PCs) [15-19]. MOFs-derived PCs have emerged as novel types of carbon-based materials that combine the well-defined shapes of the parent MOFs with high surface areas and large pore volumes [20-22]. UiO-66 is a famous zirconium based MOFs containing terephthalate linkers, which can work as both a sacrificial template and a secondary carbon precursor. During carbonization in an inert atmosphere for UiO-66, the formation of $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ nanoparticles (NPs) and PCs network can be achieved simultaneously.
Because of the growing requirement for electroanalytical chemistry, the development of efficient electrocatalysts with high catalytic activity has been proposed. Nanometer-sized noble-metal NPs based electrocatalyst has attracted considerable concern in electroanalytical application due to their unique electrical and catalytic properties [23-30].
Herein, we report the fabrication of novel Pt supported on $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCs}$ by a simple pyrolysis process using Pt species and UiO-66 polyhedrons as precursors. Pt NPs were grown on the UiO-66 surface simultaneously accompanied by their self-immobilization in the surface pores, which was employed in preparing Pt/UiO-66. Direct carbonization of Pt/UiO-66 is found to be a straight forward route for the preparation of $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ . In this strategy, the as-prepared $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ inherit the morphology of MOFs and exhibit the porosity and regular concave polyhedron morphology. It is generally known that Pt NPs exhibit electrocatalytic activity and $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ shows strong electrocatalytic performance for small molecules. In addition, PCs support, a load platform for $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ is highly promising due to the good conductivity.
As a consequence, the obtained Pt/ZrO2/PCs tri-component nanohybrids offer an attractively enhanced electrocatalytic performance for the detection of MP.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1.Chemical reagents
MP (HPLC grade), zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4}}$ , $98\%$ ), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , analytical grade), polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, MW 29-32k), N,N′-dimethylformamide (DMF, HPLC grade), Hydrogen hexachloroplatinate (IV) hexahydrate $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , analytical grade). The 0.1 M of phosphate buffer solution (PBS, $\mathrm{pH}6.0\$ ) was employed as a supporting electrolyte. All other reagents were of analytical grades, and double distilled water was used throughout.
## 2.2. Instrumentation
All the electrochemical experiments were performed with an Autolab Electrochemistry Workstation (PGSTAT 302N, Metrohm, Switzerland) with a conventional three-electrode system. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted using the Autolab electrochemical analyzer in a 0.1 M KCl solution containing $5.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ of $\mathrm{K_{3}F e(C N)_{6}/K_{4}F e(C N)_{6}}$ , from $0.1\mathrm{Hz}$ to $5.0\mathrm{kHz}$ . The sonication was made of SCIENTZ (SB-5200DT, 250 W). The nanocomposite content was carried out by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) which was performed on a PerkinElmer Diamond TG analyzer. X-Ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were obtained using a X-ray D/max-2200vpc (Rigaku Corporation, Japan) instrument operated at $40~\mathrm{kV}$ and $20~\mathrm{mA}$ with Cu Kα radiation ( $k{=}0.15406\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained by a JEM-2100F transmission electron microscope JEOL (Japan) operating at $200{\mathrm{~kV}}.$ Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy of the samples was recorded using a Nicolet Magna 560 FT-IR spectrometer with a KBr plate. The tubular furnace was made of Shanghai Shiyan Electric Furnace Factory (SK2-2-12).
## 2.3. Synthesis of Pt/UiO-66
$\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $200\ \mathrm{\mg},$ ), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -H2BDC ( $155\ \mathrm{\mg},$ and PVP (1 g) were dissolved in DMF-ethanol ( $\mathrm{50~mL}$ , $\mathrm{{v}/\mathrm{{v}=}}4;1\$ ). For optimization of the nanocomposite, different concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ ( $75~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $125~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , and $225~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ) were added into the mixture. The Pt/UiO-66 materials are named as Pt/UiO-66-1, Pt/UiO-66-2, and Pt/UiO-66-3, where 1, 2, and 3 represent the different concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ ( $125~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $175~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , and $225\upmu\mathrm{L}$ in the synthesis procedure, respectively. Then, the solution was filled in a 100 mL Teflon liner, and heated to $120^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ in drying oven. After cooling down to room temperature, the resulting product was collected by centrifugation and washed several times with fresh DMF. Thereafter, the Pt/UiO-66 was obtained by vacuum drying at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After complete dissolution, $175~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.1\ \mathrm{g\mL^{-1}\ H_{2}P t C l_{6}}$ solution was added into the mixture. In the synthesis process, $\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}{}^{2-}$ would be adsorbed into the pores and the surface of MOFs, which was reduced by ethanol. Ethanol is a mild reducing agent widely used in the preparation of Pt NPs, which is excessive in this reaction system. Therefore, almost all Pt ions precursors are converted into Pt NPs. For comparison, UiO-66 was also prepared by the same method without PVP and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ .
## 2.4. Synthesis of Pt/ZrO2/PCs tri-component nanohybrids
## 2.5. Preparation of the modified electrodes
A conventional cell was used containing a three-electrode; the glassy carbon electrode (GCE) or modified electrode served as a working electrode; an Ag/AgCl electrode and platinum electrode were reference and counter electrodes, respectively. Electrocatalyst (2 mg) was dispersed into DMF ( $\mathrm{~\textmu~mL}$ and sonicated to form a homogeneous suspension (sonication bath, 200 W). After dropping the suspension (5 μL) onto the GCE, the modified electrodes were formed and dried at $25^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of the as-prepared samples
TGA was conducted for the as-prepared samples in nitrogen to examine the conversion process during calcination (Fig.1A). In general, both the UiO-66 (a) and Pt/UiO-66 (b) weight decreases slowly between 40 and $160^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ because of the removal of chemically adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . An obvious weight loss is observed between 160 and $660^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ on the TGA curves, likely corresponding to the decomposition of the organic linkers in inert atmosphere. When the temperature reached higher than $660^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ , both the two composites showed an approximate platform, indicating the carbonization of organic moieties being completed by $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $2\mathrm{h}$ of pyrolysis.
In order to analyze the morphological character and microstructure of the as-synthesized samples, we examined the samples by TEM images. Fig.1B shows the morphologies of Pt/UiO-66 crystals in the scale of about $50\mathrm{nm}$ . It is revealed that the Pt NPs with a particle size distribution of about 5 to 8 nm were dispersed on MOFs. Angular UiO-66 nanocrystals were prepared by a benzoic acid modulated synthesis. Pt NPs were subsequently prepared by a colloidal dispersion using PVP as a stabilising agent, with subsequent deposition onto the surface of the UiO-66 nanocrystals. In the synthesis process of Pt/MOFs, the Pt precursors were reduced by ethanol, forming Pt NPs on some UiO-66 crystals. Noble-metal/MOF composites were synthesized using main three reduction methods $\mathrm{(NaBH_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and ethanol). Using $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as the reductant, the homogeneity of obtained noble-metal NPs may be improved. However, compared with the first two reducing agents, ethanol is a mild reducing agent widely used in the preparation of noble-metal NPs. Following a pyrolysis process at $800^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ , the building skeleton of Pt/UiO-66 was transferred in-situ into $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ (Fig.1C), suggesting the expediency of Pt/UiO-66 nanostructures for the fabrication of $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ composite through the morphology-preserved thermal transformation of the MOFs precursor. The HRTEM image revealed the presence of
Pt species and $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ within the $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCs}$ nanocomposite (Fig.1D). The lattice fringe shows an interplanar spacing of $0.23\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the (111) face-centered cubic structure of Pt NPs. Meanwhile, the value of interplanar spacing 0.297 nm corresponds to (101) plane of $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ .
Fig.2A shows the XRD patterns of the as-synthesized composites. The sharp diffraction peaks of UiO-66 (a) and Pt/UiO-66 (b) crystals indicated the excellent crystallinity and all the feature diffraction peaks are well matched with those previously reported [31]. As for the $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCs}$ (c) and $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ (d), following a pyrolysis process at $800^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ , the building skeleton of UiO-66 was transferred in-situ into PCs, which were decorated by small $\bar{\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}}$ crystals and Pt species.
FT-IR spectra of different materials are shown in Fig.2B. In comparison with the $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCs}$ (c) and Pt/ZrO2/PCs (d), the presence of bands at around 1436 and 1573 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ show the characteristic features of the UiO-66 (a) and Pt/UiO-66 (b), which are assigned to the symmetric and asymmetric vibrational stretching of COO- groups. The presence of characteristic bands centered at 1257 and $1661~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ can be assigned to the - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\ }$ scissoring vibrations and the characteristic $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ stretching vibrations of aromatic amines, respectively. The double bands at 3352 and $3465~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ result from the symmetric and asymmetric stretching vibration of the amine groups. The results indicate the transformation of PCs after MOFs calcination in ${\bf N}_{2}$ , which also proves the results obtained by XRD.
## 3.2. Electrochemical behaviors of MP and its detection
EIS is an influential method to provide information on transfer properties of the different modified electrodes (Fig.3). The bare GCE (curve a), UiO-66–GCE (curve b), and Pt/UiO-66–GCE (curve c) exhibit a semicircle part at high frequency, which indicated the high resistance. The diameter of the semicircle remarkably decreases for ZrO2/PCs–GCE (curve d) and Pt/ZrO2/PCs–GCE (curve e), indicating that MOFs-derived PCs materials can form good electron pathways between the electrolyte and electrode and also can be expected to be a good electrochemical platform. By fitting the data [32, 33], $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ values for different electrodes were recorded in Table S1.
Fig.S2 displays the DPV curves of Pt/ZrO2/PCs-1, Pt/ZrO2/PCs-2, and $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{PC}\mathrm{s}{}{}-3$ in the presence of MP. As for these three $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ electrocatalysts, the much higher current densities on $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCs}-2$ and 3, which can be ascribed to the more content of Pt NPs in the Pt/ZrO2/PCs tri-component nanohybrids. Compared to the Pt/ZrO2/PCs-2 electrocatalysts, response current is no significant increase for Pt/ZrO2/PCs-3. Thus, comprehensive consideration of electrocatalytic performance and economic cost, we focused on the investigation of the MP at the $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCs}-2$ (henceforth $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ for convenience).
Fig.4A displays the differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) curves of different electrodes in the presence of MP. There is a weak oxidation peak at bare GCE (a), UiO-66–GCE (b), and Pt/UiO-66–GCE (c). Obviously, an enhanced oxidation current of MP was obtained at $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCs}$ –GCE (d). Additionally, the oxidation current of MP at the $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ –GCE (e) exhibits an increased current signal, which is 9.1-, 5.6-, 5.2-, and 2.1-fold higher than that of the bare GCE, $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ –GCE, $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ –GCE, and
ZrO2/PCs–GCE, respectively (Fig.4B). The primarily reason is due to the synergistic effect of unique electrocatalytic activity of and $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ species, excellent conductivity of PCs, and good adsorption properties of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ crystals for MP. The Pt/ZrO2/PCs tri-component nanohybrids could offer a favorable microenvironment for transferring species in solution, and would also be beneficial for accelerating electron transfer between the electrode and species in solution. Therefore, we chose $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ as an electrochemical sensor for the detection of MP.
The DPV curves of different concentrations of MP are shown in Fig.5. Obviously, a series of DPV curves were obtained from the increase of the peak currents by introducing different concentration of MP into the electrolyte. The corresponding calibration curve of reduction current was shown in inset of Fig.5, indicating a linear range of $3.8{\times}10^{-9}{-}1.14{\times}10^{-2}~\mathrm{mM}$ with a limit of detection of $1.45{\times}10^{-9}\ \mathrm{mM}\ (\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}{=}3)$ . The reproducibility of the sensor was also investigated by the DPV method. The RSD of the current signal for MP $(3.8{\times}10^{-7}~\mathrm{mM})$ was less than $0.9\%$ for ten measurements using the same electrode. After being stored at $4^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for two weeks, a $12.6\%$ current loss with the Pt/ZrO2/PCs–GCE was obtained by the DPV method. The detailed comparison of MP detection property using different electrochemical sensors was demonstrated in Table 1.
Under the optimized experimental conditions, possible interferences for the detection of MP at the Pt/ZrO2/PCs–GCE were also investigated by addition of various interferences in the presence of MP (Fig.6). It shows that these possible interferences have little response when their concentrations were 10-fold higher than
MP in this potential range. The results indicated that the Pt/ZrO2/PCs–GCE has good anti-interference ability.
## 4. Conclusions
As we mentioned earlier, one of the most effective capturing and detection agent for MP among all MOFs is $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , which shows strong electrocatalytic performance and strong affinity to MP. For instance, Zhou et al. has been reported that $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ modified on self-assembled monolayer surface of gold electrode exhibited a low limit of detection of $3.04\times10^{-6}$ mM for MP [44]. In addition, nanometer-sized noble-metal combine porous carbons exhibit highly electrocatalytic activity for MP. For example, Fu et al. proposed a gold nanoparticle /carbon nanotubes electrocatalyst for MP, which provided a low limit of detection 1 $\times10^{-7}$ mM under optimal experimental conditions [35]. Thus, we have developed a simple methodology for the synthesis of highly effective electrocatalyst in which catalytically active Pt NPs are evenly encapsulated within ZrO2/PCs supports. It was prepared by using Pt/MOFs as a template and the MOFs also act as $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ and carbon sources without the need of additional precursors. Pt/ZrO2/PCs tri-component nanohybrids could offer a favorable microenvironment for transferring species in solution, and would also be beneficial for accelerating electron transfer between the electrode and MP in solution. The high performance is attributable to the synergistic effect of unique electrocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pt}$ species, excellent conductivity of PCs, and good adsorption properties of $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ crystals for MP. Therefore, an ultra-sensitive sensor for MP was developed based on the $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}$ which showed a lower limit of detection of $1.45\times10^{-9}$ mM. It is a fact that the successful fabrication of $\mathrm{Pt/ZrO_{2}/P C s}~t r$ i-component nanohybrids provides a potential approach to develop the new electrode materials for electrochemical sensors.
